      'USS Vicksburg (CL-86) had already fought her way through two of the Pacific war\'s most brutal campaigns before she ever saw San Francisco Bay. Commissioned at Norfolk on 12 June 1944 and originally laid down under the name Cheyenne, she reached the Pacific by January 1945 and went straight to the assault on Iwo Jima. Assigned to Task Unit 54.9.2, her guns conducted multiple shore bombardments between 16 and 19 February 1945 — the days of the Marine landing on that volcanic black-sand shore, when Japanese defenders fought from an underground fortress network and the casualty lists grew longer with every tide. During those operations, a Japanese Zero attacked her spotter plane; Vicksburg shot it down.',
      'Okinawa tested the ship even harder. On 18 March a Japanese G4M1 bomber fired a torpedo that narrowly missed her hull. Over the course of the campaign her anti-aircraft batteries destroyed eight Japanese aircraft including kamikazes — the guided human bombs that sank or damaged more American ships at Okinawa than at any other single operation of the Pacific war. She provided naval gunfire support throughout the land battle and escorted minesweepers through the contested East China Sea as the fighting ashore continued into June. By the time Japan announced its surrender in August, Vicksburg had been in the Pacific long enough to know precisely what the peace had cost.',
      'On 15 October 1945, Vicksburg entered San Francisco Bay as part of the Magic Carpet fleet returning veterans to American shores. The ship passed through a naval review as she approached the port — the crowd gathering at the waterfront to watch the ships come home, the same harbor that had watched them leave. She proceeded to Monterey Bay for Navy Day on 27 October before calling at Long Beach and Portland, carrying her cargo of homecoming men through the final ceremonies of a war finally over. Howard Hertzog photographed her in this harbor during that homecoming passage, the cruiser that had fought at Iwo Jima and Okinawa now completing the last leg of the war\'s long circuit.',
